  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThe weak boundary is trying to edge to our south, and the threat \nof heavy rainfall and flooding is diminishing across our area for \nthis afternoon. The flash flood watch will be allowed to expire on\ntime across the central mountains and areas adjacent, but will \nallow the flash flood watch across the south to continue as is for\nnow since the threat of flooding, while lower, is still non-zero \nespecially in areas where the ground is already saturated. \n\nWith the proximity of the nearly stationary boundary to our south, \nan inverted surface trough oriented with its axis up the Tennessee \nValley, and weak short wave energy moving across aloft during \nFriday, convection is expected to increase again across mainly over \nour south and east counties later tonight into Friday. Convective \nenergy looks very limited but may be enough for a few thunderstorms. \nSome of the CAMS suggest locally heavy rainfall will be possible \nover our far southeast counties, especially our SW NC counties. \nWhile currently the threat of flooding looks lower than what we have \nexperienced today and yesterday, there looks to be enough of a \nlocalized flooding threat to warrant mentioning in the HWO for \nFriday.\n\nThe general pattern from Saturday through Thursday will be a large \nupper ridge continuing to build to our west over the Plains and an \neastern CONUS trough.  We will see short wave energy sliding through \nthe northwest flow over our region at times which will increase \nchances for showers and storms.  The most notable short wave \nactivity is forecast to move across the area sometime in the Friday \nnight through Saturday night time frame although models differ on \nthe details.  This should result in scattered to numerous showers \nand possibly a few thunderstorms (although current model data \nsuggests CAPE will be limited at best). For Sunday through Thursday \ntiming of individual short waves is quite uncertain, but chances for \nshowers and storms with these features does generally look higher \nnorth than south.   \n\nTemperatures for Friday and Saturday look to be a bit below normal, \nthen the remainder of the period will likely see temperatures not \nfar either side of seasonal norms.",
